can we start with ukraine is labour  still foursquare behind the prime  minister  yes look we we need to see robust and  united  international action to prevent the  situation in ukraine from escalating  there is uh there are two sides to this  there is the side of ukraine which is an  argument for freedom democracy  self-determination and on the other side  you have russian aggression so we stand  four square united here within the uk  and squarely alongside our nato allies  that's exactly where we should be but of  course alongside robust action abroad we  also need to see robust action at home  from the government particularly  tackling uh illicit dirty russian money  which is flowing through the city of  london and unfortunately it seems also  flowing through the coffers of the  conservative party so i think there's a  lot more to be done to clean up our act  here at home to stop the city of london  being the destination of choice for  russian kleptocrats and money laundering  well let's take those two things in turn  let's start with uh abroad  are you at all worried that there's a  danger that  this thing could escalate inadvertently  into a situation where  there is a conflict that as the prime  minister has  said in the last 24 hours  could engulf europe  well let's hope not and there is still  time for president putin to do the right  thing  we hope the situation will not escalate  further in fact all of the efforts right  across the nato alliance are on  de-escalation as as it should be but  president putin should also be in no  doubt about the resolve of the uk on a  cross-party basis the resolve of nato  working across the multilateral alliance  to take robust action if president putin  makes the choice to escalate the  situation further  so you support you support the  deployments  of which the prime minister spoke  yesterday in the black sea and in  neighboring state to ukraine  we support the government of ukraine we  support the ukraine armed forces that's  the right thing to do and it's also  important to send a message to our  allies right across the region that the  uk is with them this is you know the  nato alliance has been the cornerstone  of western defense and democracy uh  since it was created in 1949 and and the  then labor government led by clement  atlee played a critical um role of which  we're enormously proud of in the labour  party and it's important that today  president putin understands that  alliance remains strong it is by its  nature a defensive alliance we want to  see de-escalation of the situation but  the uk stands ready to support the  government of ukraine as we're already  doing and also support nato allies right  across the region  all right well let's take the other arm  of your policy which is domestic  sanctions and  the pursuit of dirty money as you called  it  um i think you heard what uh mr  cleverley had to say do you accept his  assurances that the government really  means it this time  not really because we've had the  cross-party russia report of the  intelligence and security committee  of the house of commons which set out a  whole range of  recommendations to help clean up our act  here at home and to protect our  democracy  from russian interference that report  has not been implemented we have an  elections bill going through parliament  right now which ironically would make it  easier for foreign money to pollute our  politics we've got to put a stop to that  so  when boris johnson threatens tough  sanctions against president putin he  knows he'll have our support in  implementing those sanctions but our  message to the prime minister is that he  and his government should have acted far  sooner to clean up money laundering in  the city of london and also to clean up  the conservative parties act when it  comes to the donations it receives from  individuals linked to the russian  government  you um a couple of weeks ago your party  put out uh a list of  named individuals who you  said had given money to the conservative  party and uh you implied that they would  influence policy uh in a way  advantageous to mr putin  what evidence do you have that any of  these people  has had any influence on what the  government does  we'll take a look at the sunday papers  today which no no no whatever  you put this out two weeks ago degree  evidence that you have that's an  important thing they have any influence  whatsoever  so we know that donors have an enormous  degree of influence over the policies  and priorities of the conservative  government  that's been known for some time and has  been laid bare in today's sunday  newspapers  you've got people like the chinookans a  former deputy finance minister  in president putin's regime his wife has  given more than two million pounds to  the conservative party so i think there  are questions for them to answer on this  and  with respect if somebody said that about  is making the right decisions for the  right reasons with respect mr streeting  if somebody said that about a labour  party donor you would be using the word  smear  let me ask you again  take miss uh mr chnukin what evidence do  you have that mr newkin has had  any influence on what the government has  done what is it that he has asked for  that you can say the government has done  well today we learn that his wife who's  given significant amounts of money to  the conservative party has been  attending meetings with the prime  minister's former chief of staff lord  lister  on this advisory board for the  conservative party where donors have a  direct route into number 10 to influence  government policy so i think the  government have got serious questions to  ask about that  of course we're particularly concerned  in the context of our discussion this  morning about those individuals with  links to president putin's regime but  this applies more broadly in terms of  our politics which is that it can't just  be big money donors t